<.shell title="Spacing" current={:spacing}>
  <h1>Spacing</h1>
  <p class="lead">Eight spaces on a 4px grid.</p>

  <h2>Scale</h2>
  <p>
    Margins, padding, gaps, and sizes come from these eight. Each step is named by Tailwind's
    number for its size, so a class means what it means in Tailwind's docs: <code>mt-6</code>
    is 24px. In CSS it is the token of the same number: <code>var(--sp-6)</code>.
  </p>
  <.table id="spacing" rows={spacing()}>
    <:col :let={{step, _px, _use}} label="Class" class="w-px mono whitespace-nowrap">
      mt-{step}, gap-{step}
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{step, _px, _use}} label="Token" class="w-px mono whitespace-nowrap">
      --sp-{step}
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{_step, px, _use}} label="Size" class="w-px" align="right">{px}px</:col>
    <:col :let={{_step, _px, use}} label="Typical use">{use}</:col>
    <:col :let={{_step, px, _use}} label="">
      <span class="space-bar" style={"width: #{px}px"}></span>
    </:col>
  </.table>

  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No space is invented on a page. Only these eight numbers build, so <code>mt-5</code>
      and <code>mt-10</code>
      build nothing, and a test fails on them. If a design needs a new space,
      it joins the scale.
    </li>
    <li>
      Closer things belong together: a label sits 4px from its input, fields sit 24px apart.
    </li>
    <li>
      Widths that aren't spaces use Tailwind's named sizes, such as <code>max-w-xs</code>
      (320px), or <code>.measure</code>
      (640px) for text and forms.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.shell>
